{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"smart-evadable-teledu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/schemas/review_result.schema.json` and\n`src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json` both declare `trace_id` as\n`[\"string\",\"null\"]`, described as \"Langfuse trace id when tracing is enabled; null\notherwise\", but `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:259` and `:719` pass `trace_id=None`\nunconditionally. Nothing in rebar produces a per-run identity, so a verdict cannot name the\ntrace that produced it and no header can carry a run-stable value.\n\n## Approach\n\nMint one correlation identity per gate run at the two existing run boundaries, which already\nresolve the run's config exactly once: `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py:167`\n(plan-review, with `ctx.ticket_id` in scope) and `:762` (completion-verifier, with\n`ticket_id` in scope). Both open `gate_config(cfg)`, whose contextvar every op reads back\nthrough `resolve_gate_config` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:99-107`).\n\nAttach the identity by building a new config with `dataclasses.replace(cfg, ...)` before\n`gate_config(cfg)` rather than mutating the caller's object, so a caller-supplied config is\nnever modified in place.\n\nThe identity carries `trace_id` (32 hex characters, lowercase, from `secrets.token_hex(16)`),\n`ticket_id`, `operation`, and `run_id`. The 32-hex lowercase shape matches the W3C trace-id\nformat so a later change can supply a real OpenTelemetry trace id into the same field without\na schema or format migration.\n\nRandom per run rather than derived from the ticket id: a derived value would merge every\nre-review of a ticket into one trace, making runs indistinguishable, and the verdict's\n`trace_id` field already provides the path back from a specific verdict to its run.\n\nThis value reaches pool workers unchanged because both plan-review pools submit through\n`generation._submit_ctx`, which is `ex.submit(contextvars.copy_context().run, fn, *args)`\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/generation.py:154-165`), used by `pass1.py:426-427` and\n`container_stage.py:260`.\n\nPopulate the verdict by threading the resolved identity's `trace_id` into the existing\n`trace_id=` arguments at `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:259` and `:719`, replacing the hardcoded\n`None`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the run-identity fields on `LLMConfig` and their accessor.\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` — mint the identity at both run boundaries.\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — pass the resolved `trace_id` instead of `None`.\n- `tests/unit/test_run_correlation.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests plus a schema-validation assertion. The mechanism is contextvar\npropagation and verdict field population, both of which run for real in-process; no\nsubprocess or network boundary is involved.\n\n- Happy path: within one `gate_config` scope every `resolve_gate_config()` read returns the\n  same `trace_id`, and a produced verdict carries it.\n- Edge path: two successive scopes produce different `trace_id` values, and the value is\n  `None` outside any gate run so standalone ops are unchanged.\n- Concurrency path: a value read from inside a `_submit_ctx`-submitted pool task equals the\n  value read on the submitting thread, exercising the real `copy_context()` seam rather than\n  asserting on it structurally.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Inside one gate run, `resolve_gate_config().trace_id` is identical across repeated\n      reads; checked by a test asserting equality across three reads in one `gate_config`\n      scope.\n- [ ] Two gate runs produce different `trace_id` values; checked by a test collecting the\n      value from two successive scopes and asserting inequality.\n- [ ] `trace_id` is 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters; checked by a test asserting\n      `re.fullmatch(r\"[0-9a-f]{32}\", value)`.\n- [ ] A value read inside a `_submit_ctx` pool task equals the submitting thread's value;\n      checked by a test submitting a reader through the real `_submit_ctx` and comparing.\n- [ ] A produced gate verdict carries the run's `trace_id` rather than `null`, and still\n      validates against `review_result.schema.json`; checked by a test asserting the field\n      and running the schema validator over the verdict.\n- [ ] Outside any gate run the identity is `None` and verdicts are unchanged; checked by a\n      test asserting `trace_id is None` for a standalone op.","id":"6cd0-0786-4de8-415e","parent_id":"7e9e-07f7-8fe1-4e38","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Mint a per-gate-run correlation identity and populate the verdict trace_id"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787705216754133001,"uuid":"9d0f4002-fd5f-4c52-b967-ae00ef94d59a"}